WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.620
The current node name is Loop Over Items.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:14.920
Older articles, screenshots, and exported workflows may still use the older Split in Batches name, and the documentation still mentions that history for clarity.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.380
In this course, the current name is the one to use.

4
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:26.880
The node exists for cases where normal item processing is not enough and the workflow needs controlled iteration over items or batches.

5
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.120
The operating model is easier to reason about when the node is treated as a small state machine.

6
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.780
It receives an incoming list of items and retains that original input set.

7
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.840
On each iteration, it releases a configured number of items through the loop output.

8
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.220
The nodes connected to that output form the loop body.

9
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:52.040
After the body completes the work for the current released item or batch, the final node in the body connects back to Loop Over Items.

10
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.960
That return connection is not decorative.

11
00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:01.620
It tells the loop that the current pass has reached the point where the next item or batch can be released.

12
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:09.160
If the return connection is missing, or if it comes back before the real work is finished, the loop no longer represents the intended sequencing.

13
00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:17.260
The workflow may stop early, release later items at the wrong time, or let operations happen outside the loop body that was supposed to control them.

14
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.100
Batch Size is the main control on how many items are released during one pass.

15
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:29.480
A batch size of one creates the most controlled behavior because each iteration contains one item.

16
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.900
Larger batch sizes release groups.

17
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.120
That can improve throughput and reduce overhead, but it also changes downstream behavior.

18
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:43.700
A batch of twenty-five means the next nodes receive twenty-five items for that iteration.

19
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:50.840
Whether those nodes perform network requests serially, in batches, or through their own internal behavior depends on those nodes and their settings.

20
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.480
Engineers should treat Batch Size as a design parameter rather than a cosmetic setting.

21
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:04.020
Small batches are easier to observe, easier to retry deliberately, and less likely to overwhelm an external service.

22
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:12.680
Larger batches can reduce total execution overhead, but they can also increase memory pressure and make per-item failures harder to isolate.

23
00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:21.660
The right value depends on the external API, the workflow body, the cost of retrying, and the operator's need to understand progress during a long run.

24
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.780
The loop output and the done output have different meanings.

25
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.760
The loop output is for repeated work.

26
00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.860
It contains the current item or current batch and leads into the loop body.

27
00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.040
The done output is for work that should happen once after all input items have been released and processed.

28
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:46.820
A summary, a completion notice, a final database write, or a report generation step belongs on the done path, not inside the loop body.

29
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.180
The done path also changes how engineers think about results.

30
00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.520
During the loop, the workflow is handling per-iteration data.

31
00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:01.220
After the loop, the workflow can work with the completed set made available by the node's documented behavior.

32
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:10.760
That distinction prevents a common mistake: placing an action inside the body because it needs loop results, when the action really needs the whole processed set after the loop is finished.

33
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.280
Reset deserves careful handling.

34
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:21.460
The documented Reset option reinitializes the node with current input data during each loop and is intended for specific dynamic-loop designs.

35
00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:30.360
One documented use is a pagination pattern where an If node decides whether to query another page when the total page count is not known in advance.

36
00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.940
Reset is not a general troubleshooting switch.

37
00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:42.720
Turning it on without understanding how new input is introduced can change the meaning of the loop and can help create an infinite loop when the exit condition is wrong.

38
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.800
A good mental check is to trace one item or one batch around the full circuit.

39
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:54.220
It leaves through the loop output, passes through the body, reaches the last required operation, and then returns.

40
00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.920
Only after all releases are complete should the done path run.

41
00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:04.420
If that story cannot be traced on the canvas and in the execution data, the loop is not ready for production use.
